In Spring ecosystem, both RestTemplate and Feign Client are used for calling other microservices (HTTP APIs), but they differ a lot in style and modern usage.

🔷 RestTemplate (Traditional approach)

Spring RestTemplate

✔ What it is
Synchronous HTTP client provided by Spring.
You manually write HTTP calls (GET, POST, etc.).
✔ Example
RestTemplate restTemplate = new RestTemplate();
String response = restTemplate.getForObject(
    "http://user-service/users/1",
    String.class
);
✔ Features
Simple and direct
Full control over requests
Works in monolithic + microservices apps
❌ Drawbacks
Verbose code
No declarative style
Harder to maintain in large microservices systems
Deprecated in newer Spring recommendations (not removed, but discouraged for new projects)
🔷 Feign Client (Modern approach)

Spring Cloud OpenFeign

✔ What it is
Declarative REST client.
You define an interface, and Spring generates implementation at runtime.
✔ Example
@FeignClient(name = "user-service")
public interface UserClient {

    @GetMapping("/users/{id}")
    User getUserById(@PathVariable("id") Long id);
}
✔ Usage
@Autowired
private UserClient userClient;

User user = userClient.getUserById(1L);
✔ Features
Clean, declarative style
Integrates with service discovery (Eureka, etc.)
Built-in load balancing (via Spring Cloud LoadBalancer)
Easy to maintain in microservices
❌ Drawbacks
Slight learning curve
Less low-level control compared to RestTemplate
Needs Spring Cloud dependencies
🔥 Key Differences
| Feature               | RestTemplate            | Feign Client             |
| --------------------- | ----------------------- | ------------------------ |
| Style                 | Imperative              | Declarative              |
| Code verbosity        | High                    | Low                      |
| Maintenance           | Harder in microservices | Easier                   |
| Load balancing        | Manual                  | Built-in                 |
| Spring recommendation | Legacy                  | Preferred (Spring Cloud) |
| Complexity            | Simple                  | Medium                   |

🚀 When to use what?
Use Feign Client if:
You are building microservices
You use Spring Cloud ecosystem
You want clean, scalable code
Use RestTemplate if:
You are working on legacy systems
You need fine-grained HTTP control
Quick/simple internal calls
⚠️ Modern note

Spring is moving toward:

WebClient (Reactive) for new projects
Feign Client for declarative microservice communication



Yes — you probably heard that in context of Feign Client + service discovery / load balancing, not Feign itself directly causing port issues.

Let me explain clearly 👇

🔥 Why “port issue” comes up with Feign

Feign doesn’t call services by hardcoded ports in proper microservice setups. Instead it uses:

Service name (not IP:port)
Service discovery (Eureka / Consul)
Load balancer (Spring Cloud LoadBalancer)

Example:

@FeignClient(name = "user-service")

Here Feign expects user-service to be registered somewhere, not localhost:8081.

⚠️ When port issues actually happen
1. ❌ Direct URL misuse (common mistake)

If you write:

@FeignClient(url = "http://localhost:8081")

Then port problems can occur because:

Service runs on different port
Environment changes (dev, test, prod)
Hard dependency on fixed port

👉 This is where people say “Feign has port issue”, but actually it’s bad configuration

2. ❌ Service not registered properly

If using Eureka:

Service registered as user-service
But not actually running or registered

Feign call fails like:

UnknownHostException or Connection refused

Looks like port issue but actually:
👉 service discovery problem

3. ❌ Wrong port mapping in Docker / Kubernetes

Example:

App runs internally on 8080
But exposed as 30001
Feign tries internal name incorrectly

This causes:

Connection refused
4. ❌ Load balancer mismatch

If multiple instances exist:

One instance on 8081
Another on 8082
If registry is wrong → Feign may hit dead instance
✅ How Feign avoids port issues (correct way)
✔ Use service name only
@FeignClient(name = "user-service")

Spring handles:

Service discovery
Port resolution
Load balancing

So you never manually deal with ports.

🔥 RestTemplate vs Feign in this context
| Feature               | RestTemplate | Feign     |
| --------------------- | ------------ | --------- |
| Port handling         | Manual       | Automatic |
| Risk of port issues   | High         | Low       |
| Microservice friendly | No           | Yes       |

🎯 Interview-ready conclusion

If interviewer asks:

“Does Feign have port issues?”

You should say:

👉 “Feign itself does not cause port issues. Problems arise only when developers hardcode URLs or misconfigure service discovery or container ports.
 In proper Spring Cloud setup, Feign resolves services by name, so ports are handled automatically by the registry and load balancer.”